Now impose the invariance of light speed. A right-moving ray obeying $x=t$ must also obey $x'=t'$. Along that ray, $\begin{aligned}
x'&=\frac{(1-v)t}{F(v)},\\
t'&=\frac{(1-v)t}{G(v)}.\end{aligned}$ Equality for every point on the ray requires $F(v)=G(v).$ We may therefore write $\begin{aligned}
x'&=\frac{x-vt}{F(v)},\\
t'&=\frac{t-vx}{F(v)}.
\end{aligned}$

