p.src:
One unknown function remains. Multiply Equation  by $F(v)$: $\begin{aligned}
F(v)x'&=x-vt,\\
F(v)t'&=t-vx.\end{aligned}$ Multiplying the second equation by $v$ and adding it to the first eliminates $t$, giving $(1-v^2)x=F(v)(x'+vt').$ Similarly, $(1-v^2)t=F(v)(t'+vx').$ The inverse transformation is therefore $\begin{aligned}
x&=\frac{F(v)}{1-v^2}(x'+vt'),\\
t&=\frac{F(v)}{1-v^2}(t'+vx').\end{aligned}$

