p.src:
When $c\ne1$, dimensional consistency restores the missing factors. The ratio $v^2/c^2$ is dimensionless, while $(v/c^2)x$ has units of time. Thus $\begin{aligned}
x'&=\frac{x-vt}{\sqrt{1-v^2/c^2}},\\
t'&=\frac{t-(v/c^2)x}{\sqrt{1-v^2/c^2}}.
\end{aligned}$ The inverse is obtained by reversing the sign of $v$: $\begin{aligned}
x&=\frac{x'+vt'}{\sqrt{1-v^2/c^2}},\\
t&=\frac{t'+(v/c^2)x'}{\sqrt{1-v^2/c^2}}.\end{aligned}$ Introducing $\gamma=\frac{1}{\sqrt{1-v^2/c^2}}$ would put these in the familiar compact form, but the denominator form keeps the derivation visible.

