p.src:
Second, test a light ray. Once $c$ has been restored, a right-moving ray obeys $x=ct$, not $x=t$. Substitution gives $\begin{aligned}
x'&=\frac{(c-v)t}{\sqrt{1-v^2/c^2}},\\
t'&=\frac{(1-v/c)t}{\sqrt{1-v^2/c^2}}.\end{aligned}$ Since $c-v=c(1-v/c)$, $x'=ct'.$ The primed observer measures the same speed $c$. For a left-moving ray, substitution of $x=-ct$ similarly gives $x'=-ct'.$ The transformation treats light moving in either direction correctly.

